Most Moth blocks have a colon at the start and a semicolon at the end.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    greet |name String|:
        io.line([: Hello, [name]])
    ;
]

The colon opens the function body. The semicolon closes it.

Semicolons do not end every line. Add one only when the surrounding block is
finished. An inline block closes on its opening line. A multiline block closes
with the semicolon alone on its own line, or on the line of a terminal `else` or
terminal match arm.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    if ready:
        io.line("Ready!")
    ;
]

The `if` opens on its own line, so its semicolon stands alone after the body.

Indentation makes nesting easy to see, but it never decides scope. See
@./ (Blocks and statements advanced) for the full placement contract.

Future compiler-owned blocks use `$async:` and `$checked:`; they are deferred and
remain distinct from normal control-flow keywords and declared `name:` regions.
